  "result": "Checkout no longer throws on prices with thousands separators, and `npm test` passes (7 of 7). I haven't run it against the real server.\n\n**Root cause:** The intermittent 500s come from `parseAmount` in `src/money.js`. Its regex only accepts plain digits, so a price like `\"1,299.00\"` matched nothing. The code then read `m[1]` on `null`, which gave the `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')` in the log. Only some customers send formatted prices, which is why it looked intermittent. The log has 37 failing prices, all in one of three values: `1,299.00`, `2,450.50` and `1,005.99`.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- The parser now accepts comma-grouped whole parts, such as `1,299.00` or `1,000,000`.\n- It still rejects malformed grouping, such as `1,2` or `12,34.00`.\n- Input that isn't an amount now throws `Error('Invalid amount: <input>')`, as the function's comment already promised. Before, it crashed with a `TypeError`.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- Parsing the three prices from the log, plus a multi-million value.\n- Invalid inputs raise the descriptive error.\n- An end-to-end `checkout` test with a formatted price.\n\nI haven't checked how `src/server.js` handles errors from `checkout`. A genuinely invalid price will now throw a clear error, but it may still come back as a 500 rather than a 400.",
